"""Focused mathematics walkthroughs for topics underserved by shared examples."""
from . import T

T('0-propositions', 'equivalence-demorgan', 'Logical equivalence', 'Proving logical equivalence across every assignment', r'''
Two propositional formulas are logically equivalent when their truth values agree for every assignment to their variables. This is stronger than finding one assignment where both happen to be true. It is also different from a one-way implication: an implication can hold even when the formulas do not always agree. Write an equivalence with the symbol \(\equiv\), or show that their biconditional is a tautology.
For a small number of variables, a truth table gives a complete proof. List all \(2^n\) assignments, evaluate the inner expressions first, and compare the final columns row by row. A single disagreement disproves equivalence; complete agreement proves it. De Morgan's laws exchange AND with OR when a negation is pushed inside, and negate each operand. Parentheses matter: negating a whole conjunction differs from negating just its first operand. In the example, do not assume the law from its name; compute both sides so that the conclusion follows from all four cases.
''', 'Prove that ¬(P∧Q) is logically equivalent to ¬P∨¬Q.', r'List the cases::(P,Q)=(T,T),(T,F),(F,T),(F,F)::Two independent propositions give four assignments; none may be omitted.~Evaluate the left side::\neg(P\land Q):\quad F,T,T,T::The conjunction is true only in the first row, and its negation reverses that column.~Evaluate the right side::\neg P\lor\neg Q:\quad F,T,T,T::Both negations are false only when P and Q are both true.~Compare columns::\neg(P\land Q)\equiv\neg P\lor\neg Q::The final columns agree in every row, proving equivalence.', 'The formulas are logically equivalent.', 'Their biconditional is true on all four assignments. For P=T,Q=F, both sides are true even though P∧Q is false.')

T('0-quantifiers', 'predicate-satisfaction', 'Predicates', 'Finding the values that satisfy a predicate', r'''
A predicate is a statement template containing one or more variables. It does not have a fixed truth value until the variables receive values or are bound by quantifiers. For example, “x is greater than two” becomes true at x=5 and false at x=1. A proposition has a definite truth value; an open predicate still depends on its free variables. Always specify a domain because the possible satisfying values come from that domain.
The satisfaction set of a one-variable predicate is the set of domain elements that make it true. To determine it, translate the condition into mathematics, solve it, and check both that the candidates work and that no others were lost. Substitution then evaluates individual instances. For polynomial equations, factoring can expose every root without testing an arbitrary list of numbers. A root is a satisfying input, not a truth value itself. Distinguish the predicate P(x), the proposition P(2), and the set of all inputs satisfying P.
''', 'Over the real numbers, let P(x) mean x²−5x+6=0. Find its satisfaction set and evaluate P(1) and P(2).', r'Factor the condition::x^2-5x+6=(x-2)(x-3)::The two factors multiply to the original polynomial, so this rewrite preserves the predicate.~Solve for satisfying inputs::(x-2)(x-3)=0\iff x\in\{2,3\}::A real product is zero exactly when one of its factors is zero.~Evaluate a failing input::1^2-5(1)+6=2\ne0::P(1) is a proposition with truth value false.~Evaluate a satisfying input::2^2-5(2)+6=0::P(2) is true, agreeing with the computed satisfaction set.', 'The satisfaction set is {2,3}; P(1) is false and P(2) is true.', 'Substitution at x=3 also gives 9−15+6=0. The factorization proves there are no other real satisfying values.')

T('0-algebraic-structures', 'monoid-concatenation', 'Monoids', 'A monoid that is not a group', r'''
A monoid consists of a set with a closed associative binary operation and a two-sided identity. Closure requires the result of combining any two members to remain in the set. Associativity permits changing parentheses while preserving order. An identity must work for every member from both sides. None of these requirements says that the operation is commutative, or that every member has an inverse.
Finite binary strings provide a useful example because their operation is concatenation, not numerical addition. The empty string, denoted \(\varepsilon\), has length zero and contributes no symbols. Concatenating finite binary strings stays inside the same set, and both groupings of three strings produce the same ordered symbols. To decide whether this monoid is also a group, look for a string that can concatenate with a nonempty string to produce the empty string. Lengths add and cannot be negative, so such an inverse cannot exist. This separates a monoid's identity requirement from a group's stronger inverse requirement.
''', 'Under concatenation, do all finite binary strings form a monoid? Is it a group?', r'Check closure and associativity::(u\cdot v)\cdot w=u\cdot(v\cdot w)::Both sides put the symbols of u, then v, then w together, and the result is again a finite binary string.~Find the identity::\varepsilon\cdot u=u=u\cdot\varepsilon::The empty string preserves every string on both sides, so the structure is a monoid.~Test an inverse of 01::|01\cdot v|=2+|v|\ge2::Every candidate concatenation has positive length and therefore cannot equal the empty string.', 'It is a monoid but not a group.', 'Concatenating 0 and 1 in different orders gives 01 and 10, so associativity here does not imply commutativity; failure of the inverse for 01 suffices to rule out a group.')

T('0-algebraic-structures', 'identity-custom-operation', 'Identity', 'Finding identity from the stated operation', r'''
An identity element is a member e of the given set that satisfies \(a*e=a\) and \(e*a=a\) for every allowed a. It depends on the operation: the familiar multiplicative identity one need not be the identity of an operation written with a star. Solve the defining equations instead of guessing from the notation. A candidate must belong to the set and satisfy both sides universally; working for one convenient element is insufficient.
For an operation defined by an algebraic formula, insert an unknown e, simplify, and use the fact that the resulting equation must hold for all inputs. If the operation is commutative, the two sides have the same expression, but explicitly checking them still states what is proved. An identity, when it exists, is unique: two proposed identities must reproduce each other when combined. In this example the excluded input −1 matters because a factor involving the input is never zero, making the identity equation determine e without ambiguity.
''', 'On A=ℝ∖{−1}, define a*b=a+b+ab. Find the two-sided identity.', r'Write the identity equation::a*e=a+e+ae=a\iff e(1+a)=0::Insert e into the operation rather than assuming the usual multiplication identity.~Solve universally::a\ne-1\Rightarrow1+a\ne0\Rightarrow e=0::Every allowed a has a nonzero factor, and zero is a member of A.~Check both directions::a*0=a+0+a\cdot0=a,\quad0*a=0+a+0\cdot a=a::The proposed element works on the right and left for every allowed input.~Check closure of the operation::1+(a*b)=(1+a)(1+b)\ne0::The operation never produces the excluded value −1, so the identity belongs to a well-defined closed structure.', 'The identity is 0.', 'For a=2, 2*0=2 whereas 2*1=5; the numerical multiplicative identity one is not the identity of this operation.')

T('0-algebraic-structures', 'inverse-modular', 'Inverses', 'Solving a modular inverse and detecting its absence', r'''
An inverse of a under an operation is an element that combines with a to produce the operation's identity on both sides. In multiplication modulo m, the identity is the residue one, so an inverse solves \(ab\equiv1\pmod m\). Reducing products modulo m is essential: the inverse need not be the real reciprocal. A multiplicative inverse exists exactly when a and m are coprime. This criterion applies to the multiplicative operation, not to addition modulo m, where every residue has an additive inverse.
To find a modular inverse, use the extended Euclidean algorithm to write one as an integer combination of a and m. Reducing that identity modulo m gives the inverse coefficient. For a small modulus, checking candidate products is another valid method. Verify the resulting residue by multiplication, and state the modulus in the answer. To show an inverse cannot exist, demonstrate a common divisor that would divide every possible product but cannot divide one; merely failing to find a convenient candidate is not a proof.
''', 'Find the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 12. Does 6 have a multiplicative inverse modulo 12?', r'Find a coefficient of five::12=2\cdot5+2,\quad5=2\cdot2+1::Euclidean division reaches remainder one, confirming that 5 and 12 are coprime.~Back substitute::1=5-2(12-2\cdot5)=5\cdot5-2\cdot12::The coefficient of 5 is 5, so it gives the inverse modulo 12.~Verify the inverse::5\cdot5=25\equiv1\pmod{12}::The proposed residue reproduces the multiplicative identity.~Rule out an inverse of six::6b\equiv0\text{ or }6\pmod{12}::Depending on whether b is even or odd, the product is zero or six, and never one.', '5⁻¹≡5 (mod 12); 6 has no multiplicative inverse modulo 12.', 'gcd(5,12)=1 and gcd(6,12)=6 match the existence criterion. The product check uses identity one, not the additive identity zero.')

T('0-counting', 'unordered-committee', 'Combinations', 'Counting unordered selections without overcounting', r'''
A combination selects a subset of objects without assigning positions or roles. The selections {A,B,C} and {C,A,B} describe the same committee. For n distinct objects, the number of r-element subsets is \(\binom nr=n!/(r!(n-r)!)\). The formula assumes distinct available objects and no repeated selection of an object. Problems involving replacement or indistinguishable objects require a different counting model.
One way to derive the formula is to count ordered selections first. There are n choices for the first object, then n−1 for the second, and so on. Every unordered subset appears exactly r! times in this ordered count because its r members can be rearranged in r! orders. Divide by that multiplicity. The division is justified because every subset has the same number of orderings; it is not a general instruction to divide whenever repetition seems present. In a word problem, decide whether exchanging selected people changes the outcome before choosing permutations or combinations. Distinct committee offices would make the exchange matter.
''', 'A class has seven students. How many three-person committees can be formed when no member has a special role?', r'Count ordered selections::7\cdot6\cdot5=210::Select three distinct students in sequence as a temporary counting device.~Find the overcount per committee::3!=6::Each fixed committee is listed once for every ordering of its three members.~Divide by the multiplicity::\binom73=\frac{7\cdot6\cdot5}{3\cdot2\cdot1}=35::Removing the six equivalent orderings leaves each unordered committee once.', '35 committees.', 'Choosing the four students excluded from a committee gives the same count, C(7,4)=35. Assigning three distinct offices instead would yield 210 outcomes.')

T('0-counting', 'inclusion-exclusion-three-sets', 'Inclusion–exclusion', 'Counting a union with overlapping sets', r'''
Inclusion–exclusion counts objects satisfying at least one of several properties while correcting repeated membership. For two finite sets, \(|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\cap B|\): an object in both sets was added twice and must be subtracted once. With three sets, subtract all three pairwise intersections and then add the triple intersection. An object in all three was initially counted three times and subtracted three times, so adding it back restores one count.
Pairwise intersection sizes normally include the members who also belong to the third set. Do not interpret them as “exactly these two” unless the question explicitly says so. Draw disjoint regions or track the contribution of an object in zero, one, two, or three sets to check the formula. Once the union is known, subtract it from the stated universe to count objects with none of the properties. To find exactly one property, subtract the two relevant pairwise intersections from each set and restore its triple intersection once, then add the three disjoint single-set regions.
''', 'Among 100 students, 48 take A, 40 take B and 30 take C. Inclusive pairwise counts are |A∩B|=20, |A∩C|=15, |B∩C|=12, and five take all three. How many take at least one, none, and exactly one?', r'Add the individual memberships::48+40+30=118::This total counts students once for every subject they take, so overlaps need correction.~Apply inclusion–exclusion::|A\cup B\cup C|=118-20-15-12+5=76::Subtract pairwise overlap counts, then restore the students who were subtracted three times.~Count the complement::100-76=24::The full class partitions into students in the union and students outside it.~Count the three single-set regions::48-20-15+5=18,\quad40-20-12+5=13,\quad30-15-12+5=8::Each triple member was removed twice from its own set, so add that intersection once; the three single-set counts total 39.', 'At least one: 76; none: 24; exactly one: 39.', 'Exactly two counts are 15,10,7, totaling 32. The disjoint partition is 39+32+5+24=100, independently checking all the region counts.')

T('0-limits-continuity', 'differentiability-corner', 'Differentiability', 'Testing differentiability at a corner', r'''
A function is differentiable at a point when its difference quotient has a finite two-sided limit there. The derivative must be computed using the actual value at the point, not only a formula valid nearby. A differentiable function is continuous at that point, but continuity alone does not imply differentiability. The absolute-value function supplies a standard example: its graph has no jump at zero yet has a corner.
For a piecewise function, first check the value and continuity at the junction. Then compute the left and right derivative limits separately, or evaluate the one-sided difference quotients directly. If the two finite slopes disagree, a common derivative does not exist. A vertical tangent can also prevent a finite derivative even without unequal finite slopes. Use the derivative definition when a rule's hypotheses are uncertain; differentiating each branch away from the junction cannot by itself settle the value at the junction. In this example, the sign of the small increment determines which expression the quotient takes.
''', 'Determine whether f(x)=|x| is continuous and differentiable at x=0.', r'Check continuity::\lim_{x\to0}|x|=0=f(0)::The values approach the function value from both sides, so continuity holds.~Compute the right derivative::\lim_{h\to0^+}\frac{|h|-0}{h}=1::For positive h, absolute value equals h and the difference quotient is one.~Compute the left derivative::\lim_{h\to0^-}\frac{|h|-0}{h}=-1::For negative h, absolute value equals −h and the quotient is minus one.~Compare slopes::1\ne-1::The one-sided derivative limits disagree, so there is no two-sided derivative at zero.', 'Continuous at zero, but not differentiable there.', 'Away from zero, the derivative is −1 on the negative side and +1 on the positive side. Those branch slopes agree with the difference-quotient calculation.')

T('0-extrema-mvt', 'differentiate-product', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiating a product before evaluating its slope', r'''
Differentiation calculates the instantaneous rate of change of a function. For a polynomial, apply the power rule term by term; for a product of two varying factors, use \((uv)'=u'v+uv'\). Differentiating the factors and multiplying their derivatives generally gives the wrong result because it omits the separate contribution from each changing factor. A constant factor may instead be carried unchanged outside a derivative.
Keep the independent variable symbolic while differentiating, then substitute the requested point. Substituting the point first turns the expression into a constant and loses its local variation. Simplify the derivative if this makes its sign or slope easier to interpret. When a function can be expanded easily, differentiating the expanded form supplies an independent check of a product-rule calculation. In applications to extrema, the derivative is also used to locate stationary candidates, but a zero derivative alone does not classify a maximum or minimum. Here the goal is the derivative and a particular tangent slope, so every term of the product rule must be retained.
''', 'For f(x)=x²(x+1), find f′(x) and the tangent slope at x=2.', r'Identify the factors::u=x^2,\quad v=x+1,\quad u^{\prime}=2x,\quad v^{\prime}=1::Both factors depend on x, so the product rule applies.~Apply the product rule::f^{\prime}(x)=2x(x+1)+x^2(1)::One term accounts for the change of each factor while the other is held fixed.~Simplify and evaluate::f^{\prime}(x)=3x^2+2x,\quad f^{\prime}(2)=12+4=16::Only after obtaining the derivative do we substitute the requested point.', 'f′(x)=3x²+2x; the tangent slope at x=2 is 16.', 'Expanding first gives f(x)=x³+x², whose termwise derivative is also 3x²+2x. The tangent passes through (2,12) and has equation y−12=16(x−2).')

T('0-integration', 'substitution-inner-derivative', 'Substitution', 'Changing variables and bounds in a definite integral', r'''
Substitution is the integration counterpart of the chain rule. Choose a new variable u=g(x) when the integrand contains an inner expression g(x) together with its derivative, possibly differing by a constant factor. Then \(du=g'(x)dx\) replaces the complete differential factor. A correct substitution must rewrite the whole integral in the new variable; leaving x terms mixed with u without converting them makes the computation incomplete.
For a definite integral, convert both bounds using the same function g. This lets the final evaluation stay in u and avoids mixing original x endpoints with a new antiderivative. Alternatively, substitute back to x before using the original bounds, but keep those two methods consistent. Check that the substitution and antiderivative make sense on the integration interval, especially for logarithms and roots. When the new denominator is u and the numerator is du, the antiderivative is \(\ln|u|\); positivity on the transformed interval may remove the absolute value. Verify the answer by differentiating the antiderivative in the original variable.
''', 'Evaluate the definite integral ∫₀¹ 2x/(1+x²) dx by substitution.', r'Choose the inner expression::u=1+x^2,\quad du=2x\,dx::The numerator is exactly the derivative of the denominator, so substitution replaces it together with dx.~Convert both endpoints::x=0\Rightarrow u=1,\quad x=1\Rightarrow u=2::The definite bounds must change when the variable changes.~Integrate and evaluate::\int_0^1\frac{2x}{1+x^2}\,dx=\int_1^2\frac1u\,du=[\ln u]_1^2=\ln2::The transformed variable stays positive, and the lower endpoint contributes ln(1)=0.', 'The integral equals ln 2, approximately 0.6931.', 'Differentiating ln(1+x²) gives 2x/(1+x²). Evaluating that original-variable antiderivative at x=1 and x=0 again gives ln 2.')

T('0-conditional-bayes', 'independence-dice-events', 'Independence', 'Checking independence by comparing joint and marginal probabilities', r'''
Two events A and B are independent when \(P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)\). If B has positive probability, the same condition says \(P(A\mid B)=P(A)\): learning B does not change the probability of A. Independence is a property of the specified events and probability model. It is not established merely because two events sound unrelated, and it differs from disjointness. Disjoint positive-probability events cannot be independent, because their intersection has probability zero while the product of their probabilities is positive.
For equally likely finite outcomes, count the sample space, each event, and their intersection. Use the same underlying outcomes for every count, then compare the probabilities exactly. Events defined using the same experiment can still be independent, as the dice example demonstrates. Conversely, independent individual dice do not make every pair of events involving them independent. Identify the conditions precisely: a sum being even means the two dice have matching parity, which lets the joint event be counted without enumerating all 36 pairs one by one.
''', 'Roll two independent fair six-sided dice. Let A mean the first die is even, and B mean the sum is even. Are A and B independent?', r'Count the first event::P(A)=\frac{3\cdot6}{36}=\frac12::The first die has three even values and the second can be any of six values.~Count an even sum::P(B)=\frac{3\cdot3+3\cdot3}{36}=\frac12::An even sum arises from two even values or two odd values; those cases are disjoint.~Count the intersection::P(A\cap B)=\frac{3\cdot3}{36}=\frac14::If the first die is even and the sum is even, the second die must also be even.~Compare the product::P(A)P(B)=\frac12\cdot\frac12=\frac14=P(A\cap B)::The defining equality holds, proving independence of these two events.', 'A and B are independent.', 'Conditioning on an even sum leaves 18 equally likely pairs, nine with an even first die, so P(A|B)=9/18=1/2=P(A). The events overlap and are not disjoint.')

T('0-random-statistics', 'random-variable-map', 'Random variables', 'Building a random variable and its probability mass function', r'''
A random variable is a function that assigns a numerical value to every outcome of a random experiment. The variable is not itself an event or a probability. Different outcomes may produce the same value, so the probability of a value is the total probability of every outcome mapped to it. A discrete random variable has a probability mass function, written \(p_X(x)=P(X=x)\), whose values are nonnegative and sum to one.
Start with a clearly specified sample space and outcome probabilities. Define what number the variable records, map each outcome to that number, and group outcomes sharing a value. Then add probabilities within each group. Equal probability of basic outcomes does not imply equal probability of random-variable values, since groups may have different sizes. The support contains the attainable values; all other values have probability zero. Checking normalization verifies that no experiment outcome was omitted or counted twice. In the coin example, the two distinct ordered outcomes HT and TH both map to one head, which makes that value more likely than zero or two heads.
''', 'Toss a fair coin twice independently. Let X be the number of heads. Construct the probability mass function of X.', r'List the experiment outcomes::\Omega=\{HH,HT,TH,TT\},\quad P(\omega)=\frac14::Independence and fairness give four equally likely ordered outcomes.~Define the numerical map::X(HH)=2,\quad X(HT)=X(TH)=1,\quad X(TT)=0::The variable records the head count rather than the ordered toss sequence.~Group equal values::P(X=0)=\frac14,\quad P(X=1)=\frac24=\frac12,\quad P(X=2)=\frac14::Add the probabilities of all basic outcomes that map to each value.~Check normalization::\frac14+\frac12+\frac14=1::The groups account for the entire sample space without overlap.', 'The support is {0,1,2}, with probabilities 1/4, 1/2, 1/4 respectively; other values have probability zero.', 'The event X=1 consists of HT and TH, while X≥1 consists of HH,HT,TH and therefore has probability 3/4. This agrees with 1−P(X=0).')
